 for him deal  with uh what we call now a toxic  masculinity and maybe throughout history  were just the the the either evil or  induced evil whims of men and and I read  it and I really couldn't see myself in  it and that was very exciting and I  thought the script was excellent but  then in talking to David and realizing  there was no contest with Kenneth  Branagh or Laurence Olivier because  simply because it'd be hopeless and  because I'm a young American and and  they you know gave near flawless  performances and now we know when Dave  explained to me the language wasn't the  same here and this was the attempt at  getting a searing portrait of a young  man in circumstance that's too great for  him with a skill set that in a level of  experience and a level of a political  experience and familial experience that  is that is no good for the circumstance  at hand and then lastly was the  opportunity you know it's something I'm  really grateful for just in my career  thus far that I feel like I get to jump  into well-oiled machines and you know  DeeDee Gardner and Plan B not only do  they make some of my favorite movies but  they had put me in a movie last year  called beautiful boy and and thank you  and I guess I get to say Brad Pitt  produced both of these but that's a very  weird thing to say out loud and and and  and really I got to jump into this  Australian you know creative circus that  travels well but David and Joel but our  detour cinematographer who just [ __ ]  killed it in this movie at a markup ah  and  ben mendelsohn as well and then lastly  it'll be quiet and the youngest one here  people don't hear me but that uh that  Nicholas Patel who's sitting here makes  the most extraordinary music in movies  today if I could say that to get to do  this at a young age jump in with people  that are the best at their at their  craft I'm just going it's it's Fellig a  no-brainer Nicholas here you've got this  sort of simultaneous thing going on  where you're evoking a sense of the past  but you're doing something that's very  very contemporary very present and also  very mysterious you talk about that  strategy and also which our  collaboration with David was like  absolutely thank you very much and thank  you Timothy respect you know I'm always  fascinated with those early those first  instincts that you have when you see a  film for the first time and I watched  the film very early this year with no  music and I think the first thought I  had you know I'm not sure where this  came from but I said to myself you know  what if this were the 25th century and  not the 15th century what would that  sound like and I think there was this  feeling of these are themes that exist  for every generation to ask you know  these are questions of intellectual  fraud what's true what's false to whom  do we entrust our power a power of the  state questions that are relevant for  every era and I was just wondering like  what would a sound be that didn't  necessarily feel like it was rooted in  the early 1400s but actually could feel  like it was sometime in the future even  and that was some of the first  conversations that David and I had and  David was totally into these kind of  questions so I started experimenting  with what I thought might be very sort  of strange sounds like like looking at  the early 1400s like they were this  foreign planet so I took these bass  clarinets and played them out of their  range and ran them through this sort of  tape filter which created this almost  like strange human sound and Dave and I  spent a whole week in March in my studio  in New York where we were just really  playing with sounds taking metal and  bending it and seeing what that did but  then I did a whole deep dive on  late medieval and early Renaissance  music exploring the even the music in  England at that time John Donne stable  and like and I think it was important to  do that but at the same time David felt  very strongly that we didn'